        <body>
            <div xml:id="d165">

                <pb facs="0292" n="202" sameAs="frus:frus1920v02/pg202"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">422.11G93/1154</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in Ecuador</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Hartman</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Quito</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1920-11-22"><hi rendition="#italic">November 22,
                        1920</hi></date>.<lb/>[Received December 28.]</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 617</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honor to confirm my telegram No.
                    77, of November 20, 11 a.m.,<anchor n="27" xml:id="d165-f1"/> wherein I reported
                    total deposits to November 15, <pb facs="0293" n="203" xml:id="pg203"/> 1920, on
                    account of interest on Guayaquil &amp; Quito Railway bonds, and salt
                    certificates. The exact amount of those deposits is as follows: <table cols="5"
                        rend="width: 80%;" rendition="#center" rows="2">
                        <row>
                            <cell>Account</cell>
                            <cell>of</cell>
                            <cell>interest</cell>
                            <cell>on</cell>
                            <cell>railway bonds, S/.260,343.17</cell>
                        </row>
                        <row>
                            <cell>“</cell>
                            <cell>“</cell>
                            <cell>“</cell>
                            <cell>“</cell>
                            <cell>salt certificates, 65,263.37</cell>
                        </row>
                    </table></p>
                <p>As stated in my telegram, no remittances have been made, but I was informed on
                    Saturday by Mr. Norton that the President of Ecuador had informed him that the
                    failure to make remittance of these monies is due to the inability of the
                    Government to buy exchange on London. Mr. Norton advised me that the President
                    had assured him that no part of the sums deposited would be withdrawn, and that
                    remittances would be made as soon as drafts on London could be purchased.</p>
                <p>I have [etc.]</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Chas. S. Hartman</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
